    detailedAnswer: "Apache Parquet was originally developed jointly by Twitter and Cloudera engineers in 2012-2013. The project emerged from Twitter's need to efficiently store and query large-scale nested data generated by its social media platform. At the time, Twitter was using Apache Hadoop for big data processing but found that existing storage formats like sequence files and text formats were inefficient for complex nested data structures common in their use cases. Cloudera, a major Hadoop distributor, collaborated with Twitter to create Parquet as an open-source solution. The initial implementation was optimized for the Hadoop ecosystem, particularly for use with Apache Hadoop, Apache Pig, and Apache Hive. In 2015, Parquet became a top-level project of the Apache Software Foundation, ensuring its continued development and widespread adoption across the big data ecosystem. The collaboration between Twitter (bringing real-world use cases and requirements for nested data) and Cloudera (bringing Hadoop expertise and enterprise perspective) resulted in a robust, industry-standard file format that is now supported by virtually all major big data frameworks and cloud platforms."
