    detailedAnswer: "CSV (Comma-Separated Values) is one of the simplest and most widely used file formats for storing and exchanging tabular data. As the name suggests, CSV files store data as plain text where each line represents a row of data, and values within each row are separated by commas. The first line typically contains headers (column names), and subsequent lines contain the actual data. Despite its name, CSV files don't always use commas - the delimiter can be configured (common alternatives include tabs for TSV - Tab-Separated Values, semicolons, or pipes). CSV's simplicity and human-readability have made it a universal standard for data exchange across applications, databases, and operating systems. It can be opened and edited with any text editor, spreadsheet application (Excel, Google Sheets), or database tool. However, CSV has limitations: it lacks schema information (data types must be inferred), doesn't support nested or hierarchical data structures, has no built-in compression, and can have issues with special characters (commas or quotes within values) that require escaping. CSV files are commonly used for data import/export, configuration files, and as a simple data interchange format between different systems."
