    detailedAnswer: "JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight, text-based data interchange format that is both human-readable and machine-parseable. It excels at representing complex, hierarchical, nested data structures that would be difficult or impossible to model in flat formats like CSV. JSON uses a key-value pair structure similar to dictionaries or hash maps in programming, where values can be strings, numbers, booleans, arrays, null, or even nested JSON objects. This flexibility allows JSON to represent virtually any data structure: nested objects, arrays of objects, arrays within objects, and deeply hierarchical data. JSON is language-independent but uses conventions familiar to programmers of C-style languages (C, C++, Java, JavaScript, Python, etc.). It's widely used for web APIs (REST services), configuration files, document storage (NoSQL databases like MongoDB), and data exchange between applications. While JSON's text format makes it human-readable and easy to debug, this comes at the cost of larger file sizes compared to binary formats like Parquet or Avro. JSON is particularly well-suited for scenarios where human readability, ease of use, and interoperability are more important than storage efficiency or processing speed."
