    detailedAnswer: "Apache Spark is a unified, distributed, in-memory analytics engine designed for large-scale data processing across a cluster of computers. It is the most popular batch processing framework in the big data ecosystem today. Spark provides an interface for programming entire clusters with implicit data parallelism and fault tolerance, making it easier to write distributed applications. While Spark supports multiple processing modes, its primary and most mature use case is batch processing - processing large datasets in batch mode where the entire dataset is available before processing begins. Spark's architecture includes several key components: Spark Core (the foundational execution engine), Spark SQL (for SQL and DataFrame API), Spark Streaming (for micro-batch stream processing), MLlib (machine learning library), and GraphX (graph processing). Spark's in-memory computation capability makes it significantly faster than traditional disk-based frameworks like Hadoop MapReduce for iterative algorithms (common in machine learning and graph processing). Spark can run on various cluster managers (standalone, Apache Mesos, Hadoop YARN, Kubernetes) and supports multiple data sources (HDFS, Amazon S3, Cassandra, HBase, JDBC, etc.). It provides APIs in multiple languages (Scala, Java, Python, R). While both Apache Kafka and Apache Flink are primarily streaming frameworks (though Flink also supports batch), and Node.js is a JavaScript runtime, Spark is fundamentally a batch processing framework, making it the correct answer."
