    detailedAnswer: "A factless fact table is a special type of fact table that contains only foreign keys to dimension tables and no numeric measures or facts. While this might seem counterintuitive (after all, a 'fact' table with no facts), factless fact tables serve important purposes in dimensional modeling by capturing and tracking events, occurrences, or coverage patterns that don't have associated quantitative measurements. There are two main types of factless fact tables: event tracking factless fact tables and coverage factless fact tables. Event tracking factless fact tables capture the occurrence of events where the mere fact that something happened is what's important. Examples include: student attendance (tracking which students attended which classes on which dates), employee attendance (tracking which employees were present on which days), promotional events (tracking which promotions were run in which stores on which dates), or insurance claims (tracking which claims were filed for which policies). In these cases, the 'measure' is often implicitly 1 (the event occurred), but this count can be derived at query time. Coverage factless fact tables track the applicability or coverage of dimension combinations. Examples include: product distribution (tracking which products are available in which stores), flight schedules (tracking which flights operate between which airports on which days), or course catalogs (tracking which courses are offered by which instructors in which semesters). For coverage factless fact tables, the typical query is to identify which combinations of dimensions are valid or covered. Factless fact tables can also be used for many-to-many relationships between dimensions. The main value of factless fact tables is that they enable queries that would be impossible or very difficult with regular fact tables, such as 'show me all classes that a student attended' or 'list all products available in California stores'. While factless fact tables don't contain traditional measures, they can still be aggregated by counting rows (to get counts of events) or by using other derived measures."
