    detailedAnswer: "Data partitioning is a database design technique that divides a large table or dataset into smaller, more manageable pieces called partitions. Each partition contains a subset of the data, typically organized by partition keys (e.g., date ranges, customer IDs, or geographic regions). The primary benefits are improved query performance through partition pruning (where only relevant partitions are scanned), easier maintenance (backups, archiving, and recovery can be done at the partition level), and better parallelism (queries can process different partitions concurrently). Common partitioning strategies include range partitioning (splitting by value ranges), hash partitioning (distributing data evenly using hash functions), and list partitioning (grouping by specific discrete values)."
