    detailedAnswer: "Partition pruning is an optimization technique where the database engine automatically identifies and excludes partitions that do not contain data relevant to a specific query. For example, if a table is partitioned by date (2023-01, 2023-02, etc.) and a query filters for data in January 2023, the engine will only scan the January partition and skip all others. This significantly reduces I/O operations and improves query performance, especially for large datasets. Pruning works best with partition keys that align with common query filters. Without pruning, the database would need to perform a full table scan, which can be extremely inefficient for partitioned tables with many partitions."
