    detailedAnswer: "Data lakes and data warehouses serve different purposes in the data ecosystem. A data lake is a centralized repository that stores raw data in its native format (structured, semi-structured, or unstructured) using schema-on-read, meaning the schema is applied when data is read rather than when it is written. This makes data lakes highly flexible for storing diverse data types but requires more processing during analysis. A data warehouse, on the other hand, stores processed, structured data optimized for analytics using schema-on-write, where the schema is defined before data is loaded. Data warehouses use dimensional modeling (star schema, snowflake schema) to optimize for analytical queries. While data lakes are ideal for data scientists and exploratory analytics, data warehouses are better suited for business intelligence and reporting with predictable, structured data."
