    explanation: "OLTP handles transactional workloads (small, fast operations); OLAP handles analytical workloads (complex queries, aggregations).",
    detailedAnswer: "OLTP (Online Transaction Processing) and OLAP (Online Analytical Processing) serve fundamentally different workloads. OLTP systems are designed for operational, transactional workloads characterized by: high volume of small, fast operations (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE), high concurrency with many users, real-time processing requirements, and normalized database schemas (3NF). Examples include order processing, banking transactions, and inventory management. OLAP systems, in contrast, are optimized for analytical workloads characterized by: complex, read-heavy queries, large amounts of historical data, aggregations and multi-dimensional analysis, lower frequency of writes, and denormalized schemas (star/snowflake). Examples include business reporting, data analysis, and decision support. OLTP systems prioritize data integrity and transaction speed, while OLAP systems prioritize query performance and analytical flexibility."
