    explanation: "A materialized view stores the pre-computed results of a query physically, allowing for faster query performance.",
    detailedAnswer: "A materialized view is a database object that stores the physical results of a query as a table-like structure. Unlike regular views (which are virtual and execute the underlying query each time they are accessed), materialized views persist the query results on disk, enabling much faster access for subsequent queries. They are particularly useful for: 1) Complex, resource-intensive queries that are run frequently, 2) Aggregations and joins that would otherwise be slow, 3) Pre-computing results for dashboards and reports, and 4) Data warehouse scenarios where query performance is critical. Materialized views require refresh mechanisms to keep their data in sync with the underlying tables. Refresh can be done on a schedule (full refresh rebuilds everything, incremental refresh updates only changed data) or on-demand. The trade-off is storage space and the overhead of maintaining the materialized view during refresh."
