    detailedAnswer: "In Apache Airflow, a DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) is the core concept that defines a workflow. A DAG is a collection of tasks with directed dependencies between them, meaning the workflow can only move in one direction (directed) and cannot contain cycles (acyclic - a task cannot depend on itself, directly or indirectly). Each DAG is defined as Python code in a .py file and includes: 1) Task definitions using operators (e.g., PythonOperator, BashOperator), 2) Dependencies between tasks defined using bitshift operators (>>) or the set_downstream() method, 3) Scheduling information (cron expression or timedelta), 4) Default arguments like start_date, retries, retry_delay. When a DAG is triggered (manually or by the scheduler), Airflow creates a DAG Run, which represents an instance of the DAG's execution. Each task in the DAG Run has its own Task Instance, which tracks the task's state (running, success, failed, etc.). The acyclic nature ensures that workflows always progress forward and eventually complete."
