    detailedAnswer: "Data lineage is the process of tracking and documenting the flow of data from its origin to its final destination, including all transformations, movements, and dependencies along the way. It provides a complete picture of where data came from, how it was transformed, and where it is used. Data lineage includes: 1) Source lineage: Where the data originated (e.g., source system, file, API), 2) Transformation lineage: What transformations were applied (e.g., cleansing, aggregation, joins), 3) Dependency lineage: What processes or systems depend on the data, 4) Usage lineage: How the data is consumed (e.g., reports, dashboards, other processes). Data lineage is critical for: data governance (understanding data origins and usage), impact analysis (assessing the impact of changes), compliance (demonstrating data handling for regulations like GDPR), debugging (identifying where data issues occurred), and trust (building confidence in data quality). Tools like Apache Atlas, Collibra, and Alation provide data lineage capabilities."
