    detailedAnswer: "Type 2 Slowly Changing Dimension (SCD) is the most robust approach for tracking historical changes in dimension attributes. Unlike Type 1 (which overwrites changes) or Type 3 (which has limited history), Type 2 preserves a complete history of all changes. When a dimension attribute changes, Type 2 SCD: 1) Inserts a new row with the updated values, 2) Assigns a new surrogate key to the new row, 3) Sets the effective date to the date of the change, 4) Sets the expiry date of the previous row to the day before the change (or leaves it null if it's the current version), 5) Typically includes a Current Flag (Y/N) to identify the most recent version. This structure allows for powerful temporal queries using the effective and expiry dates. For example, you can query 'all sales for customers in California as of January 1, 2023' by joining the fact table to the dimension table where the date falls between the effective and expiry dates. Type 2 SCD is essential for: historical reporting, trend analysis, audit trails, and regulatory compliance that requires historical data preservation."
