    correctAnswer: "Ensuring data quality, security, compliance",
    explanation: "Data governance is the overall management of data availability, usability, integrity, and security, ensuring compliance with policies and regulations.",
    detailedAnswer: "Data governance is a comprehensive framework for managing data as a strategic business asset throughout its entire lifecycle. It encompasses the people, processes, policies, and technologies needed to ensure that data is: accurate, complete, consistent, secure, and available for business use. Key components of data governance include: 1) Data Quality Management: Defining and monitoring data quality metrics (accuracy, completeness, timeliness, validity, uniqueness), 2) Data Stewardship: Assigning ownership and accountability for data (data owners, data stewards), 3) Metadata Management: Documenting data definitions, lineage, and usage, 4) Data Security & Privacy: Implementing access controls, encryption, and compliance with regulations (GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA), 5) Data Lifecycle Management: Managing data from creation to archival or deletion, 6) Policy & Standards: Establishing data policies, standards, and procedures, 7) Compliance & Audit: Ensuring compliance with internal policies and external regulations, and maintaining audit trails. Effective data governance requires executive sponsorship, cross-functional collaboration (IT, business, legal, security), and the use of appropriate tools and technologies (data catalogs, metadata management, data quality tools)."
