    explanation: "A data mart is a subset of a data warehouse designed for a specific business function, team, or department.",
    detailedAnswer: "A data mart is a specialized, departmental data warehouse that focuses on a specific business function, team, or subject area. While a data warehouse serves the entire enterprise with integrated data, a data mart serves a specific group (e.g., Sales, Marketing, Finance, HR) with data relevant to their needs. Data marts can be: 1) Dependent: Created by extracting data from an enterprise data warehouse, ensuring consistency and reducing redundant ETL processes, 2) Independent: Built directly from source systems without going through a central data warehouse, which can be faster to implement but may lead to data silos and inconsistencies. Data marts use dimensional modeling (star schema, snowflake schema) and are optimized for analytical queries. Benefits of data marts include: focused scope (easier to design and implement), faster performance (smaller dataset), lower cost (less storage and processing power), and business-specific optimization. However, they can create data silos if not properly governed. In the Kimball methodology, data marts are built first and then integrated into a bus architecture, while in the Inmon methodology, data marts are derived from the central enterprise data warehouse."
