    correctAnswer: "Used consistently across fact tables",
    explanation: "A conformed dimension has the same meaning and content when it appears in different fact tables, ensuring consistency.",
    detailedAnswer: "A conformed dimension is a dimension that has the exact same meaning, content, and structure (same column names, same data values) when it is used in multiple fact tables. This ensures consistency across the data warehouse. For example, a 'Customer' dimension used in both the Sales fact table and the Service fact table should be conformed, meaning it has the same customer keys, names, addresses, and other attributes in both places. Conformed dimensions are a key principle in dimensional modeling and the Kimball methodology. They enable: 1) Consistent reporting across business processes (e.g., sales and service can be analyzed using the same customer definitions), 2) Accurate cross-fact table joins and queries, 3) Reduced development time (the dimension is built once and reused), 4) Improved data integration across the enterprise. Conformed dimensions are one of the three types of dimensions in the Kimball bus architecture, along with private dimensions (specific to one fact table) and shrunk dimensions (reduced versions of conformed dimensions)."
