    detailedAnswer: "MOLAP (Multidimensional OLAP) is an OLAP architecture that stores data in a specialized multidimensional database format rather than in relational tables. Unlike ROLAP (which uses relational databases), MOLAP uses a proprietary, optimized storage structure specifically designed for OLAP operations. Data is stored in a multidimensional cube format, which allows for extremely fast query performance for complex analytical queries. MOLAP advantages include: excellent query performance for complex aggregations and calculations (due to pre-computed aggregates and optimized storage), support for advanced analytical functions (like time series analysis, forecasting), and compact storage for sparse data (data with many zeros or null values). MOLAP disadvantages include: data is extracted from relational sources and stored separately (not real-time), limited scalability for very large datasets (due to cube storage requirements), and vendor lock-in (different MOLAP tools have proprietary formats). MOLAP tools typically pre-compute and store aggregate data at various levels of granularity, which enables fast query performance but requires additional storage and processing during data loading. Popular MOLAP tools include: Microsoft Analysis Services (in MOLAP mode), SAP BW, Oracle OLAP, and various other proprietary OLAP servers. MOLAP is particularly well-suited for: complex analytical queries, scenarios where query performance is critical, and data that doesn't change frequently."
