    explanation: "A level in a dimensional hierarchy represents a position in the hierarchy (e.g., year > quarter > month > day in a time dimension).",
    detailedAnswer: "A level in a dimensional hierarchy represents a specific position or granularity within a dimension. Dimensional hierarchies organize data into a series of levels, where each level represents a different degree of detail. For example, in a Time dimension, the hierarchy might be: Year (highest level) > Quarter > Month > Day (most detailed level). Each level contains specific members (e.g., Year 2024, Quarter Q1, Month January, Day 15). Levels are the building blocks of dimensional hierarchies and enable various analytical operations: drill-down (moving from higher to lower levels), roll-up (moving from lower to higher levels), and level-based filtering. In a Product dimension, levels might be: Category > Subcategory > Product > SKU. In a Geography dimension, levels might be: Country > Region > State > City > Store. Levels are defined in the dimensional model and are used to structure data for analysis. In OLAP systems, levels are used to: define the granularity of data, support hierarchical navigation, enable aggregate calculations at different levels, and provide meaningful labels for data. The design of levels in a hierarchy is critical for: supporting business requirements, enabling intuitive navigation for business users, and optimizing query performance."
