    detailedAnswer: "A member in a dimensional hierarchy is a specific instance or value at a particular level of the hierarchy. Members are the actual data values that populate the levels of a dimension. For example, in a Time dimension with the hierarchy Year > Quarter > Month > Day, the members might be: Year: 2023, 2024; Quarter: Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4; Month: January, February, March; Day: 1, 2, 3, etc. In a Product dimension, members might be specific products, product categories, or SKUs. In a Geography dimension, members might be specific countries, states, cities, or stores. Members have properties that define their behavior in the hierarchy: name (the display name), key (the unique identifier), parent (the member at the level above in the hierarchy), and level (the position in the hierarchy). Members can be: leaf members (members at the most detailed level with no children), parent members (members with children), or aggregated members (members that represent aggregated values). In OLAP systems, members are used to: filter data (e.g., show only sales for January), group data (e.g., aggregate sales by Quarter), and navigate hierarchies (e.g., drill down from Year to Quarter). The structure and naming of members is critical for: making the data model intuitive for business users, supporting meaningful analysis, and ensuring data consistency."
