    explanation: "A parent-child hierarchy is a flexible hierarchy where each member can have multiple parents and children, allowing for complex relationships.",
    detailedAnswer: "A parent-child hierarchy is a flexible, recursive hierarchy where each member can have multiple parents and multiple children. Unlike traditional level-based hierarchies (which have fixed levels like Year > Quarter > Month), parent-child hierarchies allow for more complex, natural relationships. Parent-child hierarchies are particularly useful for modeling: organizational structures (e.g., Company > Division > Department > Team > Employee, where a Division might have multiple Departments, and an Employee might report to multiple Teams), geographic hierarchies (e.g., Country > Region > City, where some countries might not have regions), product hierarchies (e.g., Category > Subcategory > Product, where products can belong to multiple categories), and chart of accounts hierarchies (e.g., Account > Sub-account > Detail account). In a parent-child hierarchy, each member has: a unique identifier, a parent identifier (which can be null for root members), and potentially other attributes. The hierarchy is defined by the parent-child relationships rather than by fixed levels. Parent-child hierarchies support: flexible, natural data relationships, variable depth (different branches can have different numbers of levels), and the ability to model complex organizational structures. However, they can also be more complex to: query (require recursive SQL or special functions), maintain (changes can affect large parts of the hierarchy), and visualize. Most modern OLAP systems support parent-child hierarchies and provide specialized functions for querying them."
