    detailedAnswer: "A ragged hierarchy is a dimensional hierarchy where the levels are inconsistent across different branches of the hierarchy. In a ragged hierarchy, different branches of the hierarchy have different numbers of levels or different level structures. For example, in a Geography dimension, a ragged hierarchy might have: Country > State > City (for countries with states), but Country > City (for countries without states). Another example is an organizational hierarchy where some departments might have: Company > Division > Department > Team > Employee, while others might have: Company > Department > Employee. Ragged hierarchies occur naturally in many real-world scenarios where: organizational structures vary, geographic regions have different administrative divisions, or product categories have inconsistent subcategories. Ragged hierarchies can be challenging to: model (require special handling in the dimensional model), query (require special logic to handle missing levels), and visualize (can be difficult to display in a consistent manner). However, they are necessary to accurately represent many real-world data structures. Most modern OLAP systems support ragged hierarchies and provide features for: handling missing levels, skipping levels in queries (skip-level hierarchies), and displaying ragged hierarchies in reports and visualizations. Techniques for handling ragged hierarchies include: using parent-child hierarchies, adding placeholder members for missing levels, or using specialized hierarchy types."
