    detailedAnswer: "A balanced hierarchy is a dimensional hierarchy where all branches of the hierarchy have the same structure and the same number of levels. In a balanced hierarchy, every path from the root to a leaf member has the same number of levels, and each level contains the same type of data. For example, in a balanced Time dimension, all dates would have the same structure: Year > Quarter > Month > Day, with no missing levels. Balanced hierarchies are: simple to model and understand, easy to query (no special logic needed for missing levels), efficient to store and process, and consistent across all branches. Examples of balanced hierarchies include: Time (Year > Quarter > Month > Day), Date (Year > Month > Day), and other hierarchies where the structure is consistent across all members. Balanced hierarchies are the opposite of ragged hierarchies (where different branches have different structures) and parent-child hierarchies (where relationships are defined by parent-child links rather than fixed levels). Balanced hierarchies are particularly well-suited for: time-based dimensions, standardized organizational structures, and other scenarios where consistency is important. They are easier to work with in: queries (no need for special handling of missing levels), reports (consistent display across all branches), and visualizations (uniform structure for all members)."
