    detailedAnswer: "A skip-level hierarchy is a dimensional hierarchy that allows for querying at non-consecutive levels, effectively skipping intermediate levels in the hierarchy. Skip-level hierarchies are useful when you want to analyze data at a higher level without including all the intermediate levels. For example, in a Time hierarchy with levels Year > Quarter > Month > Day, a skip-level query might go directly from Year to Day, skipping Quarter and Month. Skip-level hierarchies are particularly useful for: answering specific business questions that don't require all levels of detail, reducing the complexity of queries, and improving query performance by avoiding unnecessary aggregations. In a skip-level hierarchy, the database or OLAP system must be able to: handle queries that skip levels, maintain the correct relationships between levels, and ensure accurate aggregations across skipped levels. Skip-level hierarchies are different from ragged hierarchies (where different branches have different structures) and balanced hierarchies (where all branches have the same structure). They are a feature of many modern OLAP systems and are supported by various query languages and tools. Skip-level hierarchies are commonly used in: time-based analysis (e.g., comparing yearly data without breaking it down by quarter or month), geographic analysis (e.g., comparing country-level data without breaking it down by region or state), and organizational analysis (e.g., comparing division-level data without breaking it down by department or team)."
