    detailedAnswer: "A multiple hierarchy is a dimension that contains more than one hierarchy, allowing for different ways to categorize or analyze the same data. Multiple hierarchies are useful when a dimension can be organized in different ways depending on the business requirement or analytical need. For example, a Product dimension might have: a Category hierarchy (Electronics > Computers > Laptops), a Supplier hierarchy (Supplier A > Supplier B > Product X), and a Geographic hierarchy (North America > USA > California > Product Y). Similarly, a Customer dimension might have: a Geographic hierarchy (Country > Region > City), a Segment hierarchy (Enterprise > Mid-market > Small Business), and a Sales Rep hierarchy (Sales Team > Sales Rep > Customer). Multiple hierarchies enable: different analytical perspectives on the same data, flexibility in reporting and analysis, and the ability to answer diverse business questions. Multiple hierarchies are different from: level-based hierarchies (which have a single path from root to leaf), parent-child hierarchies (which define relationships through parent-child links), and ragged hierarchies (where different branches have different structures). Supporting multiple hierarchies can be challenging in: data modeling (requires careful design to avoid redundancy), data loading (requires processes to populate all hierarchies), and query performance (can increase complexity and resource usage). However, they are a powerful feature for providing flexible analytical capabilities."
