    detailedAnswer: "A recursive hierarchy is a dimensional hierarchy where members can be parents of themselves, either directly or indirectly, creating cycles in the hierarchy. Recursive hierarchies are rare in business applications but can occur in certain scenarios, such as: self-referencing relationships (e.g., an Employee who manages themselves), circular dependencies (e.g., Product A contains Product B, which contains Product A), and other scenarios where members have circular relationships. Recursive hierarchies are problematic because they: create infinite loops in the hierarchy, make it impossible to define a root member or leaf members, cause query performance issues (queries can enter infinite loops), and are difficult to visualize and understand. Most OLAP systems and dimensional modeling methodologies: do not support recursive hierarchies, or provide special handling to prevent cycles. Techniques for handling potential recursive hierarchies include: adding constraints to prevent cycles, using parent-child hierarchies with validation rules, or restructuring the data model to avoid recursion. In practice, recursive hierarchies are usually an indication of a data modeling error or a problem with the source data that needs to be addressed."
