    detailedAnswer: "Indexing strategy in data warehouses is the process of designing and implementing an effective indexing approach to optimize query performance. Indexing strategy is critical for data warehouses because they typically handle: large datasets, complex analytical queries, and various query patterns. A good indexing strategy involves: 1) Choosing the right columns to index: focusing on columns used in WHERE clauses, JOIN conditions, and ORDER BY clauses, 2) Selecting the appropriate index type: B-tree indexes for range queries and sorting, bitmap indexes for low-cardinality columns with many repeated values, hash indexes for equality queries, 3) Balancing index benefits with overhead: considering the storage space and maintenance overhead of each index, 4) Avoiding redundant indexes: not creating multiple indexes that serve the same purpose, 5) Considering composite indexes: creating indexes on multiple columns for queries that filter or sort on multiple columns, 6) Using covering indexes: creating indexes that contain all the columns needed for a query to avoid table access. In data warehouses, common indexing strategies include: bitmap indexing for dimension tables (due to low cardinality), B-tree indexing for fact tables (for range queries), and hash indexing for specific equality queries. The indexing strategy should be: tailored to the specific query patterns and workload of the data warehouse, regularly reviewed and updated as query patterns change, and tested to ensure that it improves rather than degrades performance. Poor indexing strategy can lead to: slower query performance, increased storage requirements, and longer data loading times."
