    detailedAnswer: "B-tree indexing is a database indexing technique that uses a balanced tree structure to efficiently store and retrieve data. The 'B' in B-tree stands for balanced, indicating that all leaf nodes are at the same level in the tree. B-tree indexes are the most common type of index in relational databases and are the default index type in most database systems. In a B-tree index, data is organized in a hierarchical tree structure where: each node contains a range of values and pointers to child nodes, leaf nodes contain the actual index entries, and the tree is balanced (all paths from the root to leaf nodes have the same length). B-tree indexes are particularly effective for: range queries (finding all values within a range), equality queries (finding exact matches), sorting (retrieving data in sorted order), and join operations. B-tree indexes provide several advantages: balanced structure ensures consistent performance, efficient for both point queries and range queries, and support for sorted data retrieval. B-tree indexes are used in: most relational database systems (Oracle, SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL), transactional databases (OLTP), and read-write workloads. B-tree indexes are the default choice for most indexing scenarios and are well-suited for general-purpose database applications. However, they can be less efficient than bitmap indexes for low-cardinality columns and may require more storage space than hash indexes."
