    detailedAnswer: "Range partitioning is a data partitioning strategy that divides data into partitions based on value ranges. In range partitioning, each partition contains data within a specific range of values for one or more columns. For example, a table partitioned by date might have partitions for: 2023-01 to 2023-03, 2023-04 to 2023-06, 2023-07 to 2023-09, and 2023-10 to 2023-12. Range partitioning is particularly useful for: time-based data (e.g., dates, timestamps), numeric data with natural ranges (e.g., price ranges, age ranges), and any data that can be logically divided into ranges. Range partitioning provides several benefits: natural organization of data based on value ranges, efficient partition pruning for range queries (e.g., selecting data within a specific date range), and good performance for range-based queries. However, range partitioning also has some limitations: potential for uneven data distribution (if ranges are not chosen carefully), difficulty in adding new partitions (may require reorganization of existing data), and the need to define appropriate range boundaries. Range partitioning is commonly used in data warehouses for: fact tables partitioned by date, dimension tables with natural ranges, and other scenarios where range-based partitioning makes sense. It is supported by most major database systems, including: Oracle, SQL Server, PostgreSQL, and MySQL."
