    detailedAnswer: "Round-robin partitioning is a data partitioning strategy that distributes data evenly across all partitions in a circular, round-robin fashion. In round-robin partitioning, each new row is assigned to the next partition in sequence, regardless of the row's values. Once the last partition is reached, the next row goes to the first partition, and the cycle repeats. Round-robin partitioning is particularly useful for: data without a natural partitioning key, scenarios where even data distribution is important, and tables where all partitions are expected to have similar characteristics. Round-robin partitioning provides several benefits: simple and easy to implement, even distribution of data across partitions, and no need to define partitioning keys or ranges. However, round-robin partitioning also has some limitations: not suitable for partition pruning (since data is not organized by value), potential for hotspots (if data is inserted in bursts), and less efficient for range or value-based queries. Round-robin partitioning is less commonly used in data warehouses than range, hash, or list partitioning, but it can be useful in certain scenarios. It is supported by some database systems, but may not be available in all databases."
