    detailedAnswer: "Data clustering in data warehouses is the process of physically co-locating related data to improve query performance. Data clustering involves organizing data on disk so that related rows are stored close to each other, reducing the amount of I/O required to retrieve related data. Data clustering is particularly useful for: queries that access related data (e.g., all rows with the same partitioning key), scenarios where reducing I/O is important for performance, and tables with natural groupings of data. Common clustering strategies include: clustered indexes (in some database systems), table clustering (physically ordering rows based on one or more columns), and partition clustering (co-locating related partitions). Data clustering provides several benefits: improved query performance by reducing I/O operations, better cache utilization by keeping related data together, and more efficient data retrieval for analytical queries. However, data clustering also has some limitations: increased complexity in data loading and maintenance, potential for performance degradation for certain query patterns, and the need to carefully choose clustering keys. Data clustering is commonly used in data warehouses for: fact tables clustered by common query columns, dimension tables clustered by primary keys, and other scenarios where co-locating related data can improve performance. It is supported by various database systems, but the specific implementation may vary."
