    correctAnswer: "Caching query results to avoid recomputation",
    explanation: "Query caching stores the results of frequently executed queries, allowing subsequent identical queries to retrieve results from cache instead of recomputing.",
    detailedAnswer: "Query caching in data warehouses is a technique for improving query performance by storing the results of frequently executed queries and reusing them for subsequent identical queries. Query caching works by: capturing the results of a query when it is first executed, storing the results in a cache (memory or disk), and returning the cached results for subsequent identical queries instead of re-executing the query. Query caching is particularly useful for: frequently executed queries, queries with expensive computations or aggregations, and scenarios where the same query is executed multiple times with the same parameters. Query caching provides several benefits: improved query performance by eliminating redundant query execution, reduced resource utilization (CPU, memory, I/O), and better scalability by reducing the load on the database. However, query caching also has some limitations: cache invalidation (cached results may become stale if underlying data changes), memory overhead (caching requires additional memory), and the need to carefully manage cache sizes and policies. Query caching can be implemented at various levels: database level (built-in query caching in database systems), application level (caching in application code), or middle-tier level (caching in middleware or BI tools). Most modern database systems support some form of query caching, and many BI tools also provide caching capabilities."
