    detailedAnswer: "Materialized view refresh is the process of updating a materialized view with the latest data from the underlying tables. Materialized views store the pre-computed results of a query, allowing for faster query performance. However, because the data in the underlying tables can change, materialized views need to be refreshed periodically to ensure that they contain current data. Materialized view refresh can be performed in several ways: 1) Full refresh: Rebuilds the materialized view from scratch by re-executing the underlying query, 2) Incremental refresh: Updates the materialized view with only the data that has changed since the last refresh, 3) Fast refresh: Updates the materialized view incrementally using change logs or triggers, 4) On-demand refresh: Refreshes the materialized view when explicitly requested by a user or application, 5) Scheduled refresh: Refreshes the materialized view at regular intervals based on a schedule. Materialized view refresh provides several benefits: ensures that materialized view data is up-to-date and consistent with underlying tables, enables fast query performance by using pre-computed results, and reduces the load on the database by avoiding redundant computations. However, materialized view refresh also has some limitations: overhead for refreshing large materialized views, potential for data inconsistency if not refreshed frequently enough, and the need to manage refresh schedules and strategies. Materialized view refresh is a critical aspect of materialized view management and is supported by most major database systems."
