    detailedAnswer: "VACUUM in Delta Lake is a maintenance command that permanently removes data files that are no longer referenced by any active version of the table, thereby reclaiming storage space. Delta Lake maintains a transaction log that allows for time travel querying - the ability to query the table as it existed at a previous point in time. By default, Delta Lake retains 30 days worth of transaction history (configurable via the delta.logRetentionDuration property). When you run VACUUM, Delta Lake identifies files that are older than the retention period and no longer needed for time travel. These files are then deleted from storage. The VACUUM command is crucial for storage cost management because without it, old versions of files would continue to consume storage space even after they are no longer queryable. Important considerations for VACUUM: it is irreversible - once files are removed by VACUUM, they cannot be recovered, it breaks time travel capabilities for versions older than the retention period, and it requires the DRY RUN option first to preview what will be deleted before actually executing. The command can be run with different retention periods based on your storage and compliance requirements."
