    detailedAnswer: "Apache Iceberg was originally developed as an open table format by engineers at Netflix and Apple. Netflix began developing Iceberg in 2017 to address limitations they encountered with existing table formats in their data lake environment. Netflix needed a table format that could handle: petabyte-scale datasets with billions of files, frequent updates and deletes on large datasets, reliable schema and partition evolution without downtime, and time travel capabilities to query historical data and recover from mistakes. Around the same time, Apple was facing similar challenges with their data infrastructure at scale. Rather than developing separate solutions, Netflix and Apple collaborated to create Iceberg as a shared, community-driven project. In 2018, Netflix open-sourced the project, and in 2020, Iceberg was accepted into the Apache Incubator program. The collaboration between Netflix and Apple on Iceberg is notable because both companies contributed significant engineering resources and real-world use cases that shaped the designs focus on scalability, reliability, and usability. This joint development ensured that Iceberg would meet the needs of enterprise-scale data environments from day one. Today, Iceberg has grown into a vibrant open-source community with contributors from many organizations and has become one of the leading open table formats alongside Delta Lake and Apache Hudi."
