    correctAnswer: "Copy-On-Write table type",
    explanation: "COW (Copy-On-Write) is a table type in Hudi where updates create new file versions and old files are deleted.",
    detailedAnswer: "COW (Copy-On-Write) is one of the two table types available in Apache Hudi, with the other being MOR (Merge-On-Read). The COW approach prioritizes read performance and is designed for scenarios where query performance is more critical than write performance. In COW tables, every write operation (insert, update, or delete) creates new versions of the affected data files rather than modifying existing ones. This immutable approach ensures data consistency and enables several important capabilities: ACID transactions by ensuring that each write operation is atomic and isolated, Time travel by allowing queries against historical versions of the data, and Efficient reads because the current view of the data is always stored in complete, up-to-date files. When data is updated in a COW table, Hudi reads the existing data files, applies the updates or deletes in memory, and writes the results as new Parquet files. The old files are then marked for deletion and removed during cleanup processes. This approach ensures that queries always read from complete, self-contained Parquet files, which maximizes read performance and compatibility with standard query engines. The COW approach is particularly beneficial for: read-heavy workloads where query performance is critical, use cases requiring strong consistency guarantees, and scenarios where data is primarily appended with infrequent updates. The trade-off with COW is that write operations can be slower and more resource-intensive than MOR because they require reading, modifying, and rewriting data files. However, COW tables generally provide better read performance and are simpler to work with because they do not require merging delta files at query time."
