    explanation: "Data lineage tracks the flow of data from source to destination, including all transformations and movements.",
    detailedAnswer: "Data lineage in a data lake is the ability to track and visualize the complete history of data as it flows from its source systems through various transformations, storage locations, and consumption points. It provides a comprehensive view of where data comes from, how it has been processed and transformed, and where it ends up in the data ecosystem. There are several types of data lineage that are important in a data lake: Technical lineage that tracks the physical flow of data including file movements, table transformations, and ETL/ELT processes, Business lineage that tracks the logical flow of data including business processes, data relationships, and semantic transformations, and Operational lineage that tracks the operational context including job execution details, timing information, and system dependencies. Data lineage typically includes information such as: source systems and their connection details, extraction processes and schedules, data transformation logic and rules, intermediate storage locations and formats, data quality checks and validation rules, and target systems and consumption points. Effective data lineage in a data lake provides several important benefits: enhanced data transparency by making it clear where data comes from and how it has been processed, improved data trust by enabling users to verify data accuracy and understand its journey through the system, better impact analysis by understanding how changes to source systems or processes will affect downstream systems, simplified troubleshooting by quickly identifying the source of data quality issues or inconsistencies, and regulatory compliance by demonstrating data provenance and audit trails for compliance requirements. Data lineage is particularly important in data lake environments because: data often comes from multiple, disparate sources, data undergoes complex transformations through multiple processing stages, and data is consumed by various downstream systems and users. Common approaches to implementing data lineage include metadata capture during ETL processes, automated lineage extraction from processing code, and visualization tools that display lineage as interactive diagrams."
