    detailedAnswer: "Compaction in a data lake is the process of combining many small files into fewer, larger files to improve query performance and reduce storage overhead. This is particularly important in data lake environments where the small file problem can significantly degrade performance. The small file problem occurs when there are thousands or even millions of small files, each containing only a small amount of data. While each file may be individually small, collectively they can cause major performance issues due to: high metadata overhead as each file requires its own metadata entry in the file system, increased I/O operations because each file requires a separate read operation, and memory pressure as query engines need to manage many file handles and metadata in memory. Compaction addresses these issues by merging small files into larger ones, typically in the range of 100MB to 1GB depending on the specific requirements and configuration. The compaction process works by: identifying small files that meet certain criteria (typically based on file size and age), grouping these files together based on their partition or other logical grouping, reading the data from the small files, merging them into larger files while maintaining the same data format and structure, and writing the new, larger files back to storage. In Delta Lake, compaction is performed using the OPTIMIZE command, which can be run manually or scheduled to run automatically. Other data lake formats may have similar optimization commands. The benefits of compaction include: improved query performance by reducing the number of files that need to be read for each query, reduced metadata overhead by decreasing the number of metadata entries, lower memory usage as query engines need to manage fewer file handles, and better storage efficiency by eliminating the overhead of many small files. Compaction is typically configured with parameters such as the target file size, the minimum file size to be considered for compaction, and the frequency of compaction operations."
