    detailedAnswer: "Parquet format is an open-source columnar storage file format specifically designed and optimized for analytical workloads in big data environments. It was developed jointly by Cloudera and Twitter and has become one of the most popular file formats for data lakes due to its efficiency, performance, and compatibility with major big data processing frameworks. Parquet stores data in a columnar format, meaning all values for a particular column are stored together in contiguous memory rather than storing all columns for a single row together. This organization is particularly efficient for analytical queries that typically read only a subset of columns from a table. Key features and characteristics of Parquet include: high performance for analytical queries by enabling column pruning (reading only the columns needed for a query) and predicate pushdown (filtering data at the storage layer), excellent compression ratios due to the columnar organization which allows for efficient compression techniques like run-length encoding and dictionary encoding, support for nested and complex data structures including arrays, maps, and nested records, making it suitable for hierarchical data, schema evolution capabilities that allow the schema to change over time without requiring data to be rewritten, and compatibility with virtually all major big data processing frameworks including Apache Spark, Hadoop MapReduce, Hive, Presto, Drill, and many others. Parquet files are self-describing, meaning they contain both the data and the schema information within the file itself. This makes Parquet files portable and easy to share between different systems and organizations. The format also supports various compression codecs including Snappy (default), Gzip, LZO, and Zstandard, allowing users to choose the appropriate balance between compression ratio and compression/decompression speed based on their specific requirements. Parquet is widely used in data lakes for storing data from various sources and is particularly well-suited for: large-scale analytical workloads, data warehouse-style queries, and batch processing jobs. It is also increasingly used for real-time analytics and streaming workloads due to its efficiency and performance characteristics."
