    correctAnswer: "Filtering data at the storage layer before reading",
    explanation: "Predicate pushdown pushes filter conditions down to the storage layer, allowing files or partitions to be skipped entirely during reads.",
    detailedAnswer: "Predicate pushdown in a data lake is a powerful query optimization technique that pushes filter conditions down to the storage layer, allowing files, partitions, or even individual row groups to be skipped entirely during read operations. This optimization can dramatically improve query performance by reducing the amount of data that needs to be read from storage and processed by the query engine. The basic principle of predicate pushdown is that when a query includes a WHERE clause with filter conditions, instead of reading all the data and then applying the filters in memory, the query engine analyzes the predicates and determines which parts of the data can be skipped based on metadata and statistics stored with the data. For example, if data is partitioned by date and a query includes WHERE date = '2024-01-15', predicate pushdown allows the query engine to skip reading all partitions except the one for January 15, 2024. This is similar to partition pruning but works at a more granular level within the files themselves. Modern columnar file formats like Parquet and ORC support predicate pushdown at different levels: at the file level by skipping entire files that cannot contain matching data, at the row group level by skipping row groups within files (Parquet files are divided into row groups, typically containing 1 million rows each), and at the page level by skipping individual data pages within row groups. The effectiveness of predicate pushdown depends on having rich metadata and statistics stored with the data. These typically include: min/max values for each column within each row group, which allows the query engine to determine if a row group could possibly contain values matching the predicate, null counts which indicate how many null values are present, and bloom filters which are probabilistic data structures that can quickly determine if a partition or row group might contain matching data. The benefits of predicate pushdown include: dramatically reduced I/O operations by only reading data that is relevant to the query, improved query performance by reducing the amount of data that needs to be processed, lower resource utilization including CPU, memory, and network resources, and cost savings in cloud environments where storage I/O operations are metered. Predicate pushdown works particularly well in combination with other optimizations like partition pruning and column pruning, and it is automatically applied by most modern query engines including Apache Spark, Hive, Presto, and cloud-based services like AWS Athena and Google BigQuery."
