    correctAnswer: "Unified data lake and warehouse platform",
    explanation: "Databricks Lakehouse Platform provides a unified architecture that combines the best of data lakes and data warehouses.",
    detailedAnswer: "Databricks Lakehouse is a unified data management platform that combines the best features of data lakes and data warehouses into a single, integrated architecture. It was introduced by Databricks to address the limitations of traditional data architectures that force organizations to choose between the flexibility and scalability of data lakes and the performance and reliability of data warehouses. The Lakehouse architecture provides a single platform that supports both data lake use cases (such as storing massive amounts of raw data in various formats, supporting diverse data types, and enabling data science and machine learning) and data warehouse use cases (such as providing ACID transactions, strong consistency, SQL analytics, and reliable performance). Key features and characteristics of Databricks Lakehouse include: open file formats with Delta Lake as the foundational storage layer, built on open formats like Parquet and optimized for both batch and streaming workloads, ACID transactions that ensure data integrity and consistency through Delta Lakes transaction log, which records all changes to the data, unified governance and security through Unity Catalog which provides a centralized governance solution for data, security, and compliance across the entire data estate, support for both SQL and data science workloads enabling both analysts and data scientists to work on the same platform using their preferred tools and languages, and high performance through optimizations like Photon (vectorized query engine) and Auto Loader (incremental file ingestion). The Databricks Lakehouse Platform is built on top of cloud storage services like AWS S3, Azure Data Lake Storage, or Google Cloud Storage, and it uses Delta Lake as its storage layer. Delta Lake provides the foundation for the Lakehouse by adding reliability and performance features to data lakes. These include: ACID transactions for ensuring data consistency, time travel for querying historical versions of data, schema enforcement and evolution for maintaining data quality, and optimized performance for analytical queries. The Lakehouse architecture provides several important benefits: elimination of data silos by providing a single platform for all data types and use cases, reduced complexity by eliminating the need to maintain separate data lake and data warehouse systems, improved performance through optimized storage formats and query engines, and enhanced reliability through ACID transactions and robust data management features. Databricks Lakehouse supports various workloads including: data engineering for building data pipelines and transformations, analytics for SQL-based analysis and reporting, data science for machine learning and statistical analysis, and business intelligence for dashboards and visualization. The platform provides native integrations with various tools and frameworks, and it supports industry standards for data formats, query languages, and APIs."
