    detailedAnswer: "Apache Spark is a unified, open-source analytics engine designed for large-scale distributed data processing across computer clusters. Originally developed at UC Berkeley's AMPLab in 2009 and later donated to the Apache Software Foundation, Spark has become one of the most popular big data processing frameworks. Unlike traditional disk-based frameworks like Hadoop MapReduce, Spark performs in-memory computation, which dramatically improves performance for iterative algorithms (common in machine learning and graph processing) by keeping data in memory across multiple operations. Spark's unified nature means it provides a single, integrated solution for batch processing, real-time streaming, machine learning, graph processing, and SQL queries through its various components. It supports multiple programming languages including Scala, Java, Python (via PySpark), and R (via SparkR), making it accessible to a wide range of developers and data scientists. Spark can run standalone or on cluster managers like Apache Mesos, Hadoop YARN, or Kubernetes, and can access data from various sources including HDFS, Apache Cassandra, Apache HBase, Amazon S3, and relational databases via JDBC."
