    detailedAnswer: "Apache Spark's architecture is organized into several tightly integrated components that work together to provide a comprehensive data processing platform. Spark Core serves as the foundational execution engine, providing the distributed task dispatching, scheduling, and basic I/O functionalities that all other components build upon. Spark SQL is the module for working with structured and semi-structured data, providing a DataFrame API and the ability to execute SQL queries directly on Spark data; it includes Catalyst, a sophisticated query optimizer, and Tungsten, a binary processing engine that dramatically improves performance. Spark Streaming enables the processing of real-time data streams by dividing the stream into micro-batches that are processed using Spark's standard batch processing engine, achieving exactly-once fault-tolerant processing guarantees. MLlib is Spark's scalable machine learning library that provides both high-quality algorithms (classification, regression, clustering, collaborative filtering, etc.) and the ability to create complex ML pipelines using a simple, declarative syntax. GraphX is the graph processing library that extends Spark to support graph computation, providing algorithms for page rank, connected components, and triangle counting, as well as a flexible API for building custom graph algorithms. These components can be used independently or combined within the same application, and they all share Spark Core's distributed execution engine."
