    detailedAnswer: "The variable block in Terraform defines input variables that allow users to customize aspects of their Terraform configuration without modifying the main configuration files. This makes configurations more reusable, flexible, and maintainable. Variables can be defined with a name, optional description, optional type constraints, optional default values, and validation rules. Variables can be provided through several methods, in order of precedence: Command-line flags (-var or -var-file), Environment variables (TF_VAR_<name>), A terraform.tfvars or .auto.tfvars file in the current directory, A terraform.tfvars file specified with -var-file. Common use cases for variables include parameterizing resource names, sizes, or configurations for different environments (development, staging, production), making configurations portable across different teams or projects, and allowing for customization without changing the core infrastructure definition. Type constraints allow you to specify the expected type of a variable (string, number, bool, list, map, etc.), which helps catch configuration errors early. Validation rules can be added to ensure that variable values meet specific requirements before they are used. Variables are referenced in the configuration using the var.<NAME> syntax."
