    detailedAnswer: "A Terraform module is a collection of Terraform configuration files (with .tf or .tf.json extensions) that are grouped together in a directory. Modules are the primary way to package and reuse Terraform configuration across different projects and organizations. A module can be as simple as a single directory with a few resources, or as complex as an entire multi-resource configuration that manages a complete architecture. Every Terraform configuration has at least one module, known as the root module, which consists of all the .tf files in the main working directory. Modules can call other modules, allowing for hierarchical composition of infrastructure. There are two types of modules: root modules (the main configuration directory) and child modules (which can be called from other modules). Child modules can be loaded from local paths or from remote sources like GitHub, Bitbucket, GitLab, Mercurial, S3 buckets, or the Terraform Registry. Using modules provides several benefits: Code reuse across multiple projects, Organization of complex infrastructure into logical components, Encapsulation of implementation details behind a simpler interface, Versioning of infrastructure components, and Sharing of configurations across teams. When a module is called, its resources are treated as part of the calling module's configuration, and their state is stored in the same state file."
