    correctAnswer: "Framework for building data pipelines to integrate Kafka with external systems",
    explanation: "Kafka Connect is a framework for building data pipelines that integrate Kafka with external systems like databases, file systems, and cloud services.",
    detailedAnswer: "Kafka Connect is a framework included with Apache Kafka that provides a standardized way to build and manage data pipelines for integrating Kafka with external systems. It is designed to make it easier to move data in and out of Kafka by providing a common framework for developing connectors that can read from (source connectors) or write to (sink connectors) various data systems. Kafka Connect provides several key benefits: Standardization of connector development through a common API and framework, Centralized management and configuration of connectors through a REST API, Scalability through distributed workers that can run multiple connectors in parallel, Fault tolerance through automatic detection and recovery from connector failures, and Offset management for tracking data position in both Kafka topics and external systems. Kafka Connect runs as a service (the Connect worker) that manages the execution of connectors. Connectors are plugins that implement the actual data movement logic, and they can be developed by anyone and published for others to use. Kafka provides many built-in connectors for common data sources and sinks, and there is a vibrant ecosystem of community-developed connectors. Kafka Connect supports two modes of operation: standalone mode (single worker, suitable for development and testing) and distributed mode (multiple workers, suitable for production). Connectors are configured using JSON files that specify the connector class, connection details, and other parameters. The Connect REST API allows for dynamic management of connectors, including adding, removing, and reconfiguring connectors without restarting the service."
