    correctAnswer: "Library for building stream processing applications with Kafka",
    explanation: "Kafka Streams is a client library for building stream processing applications that consume from and produce to Kafka topics.",
    detailedAnswer: "Kafka Streams is a client library for building real-time stream processing applications that consume from, process, and produce to Apache Kafka topics. It is a lightweight library that can be integrated into any Java application, allowing developers to build powerful stream processing applications using simple, functional APIs. Kafka Streams provides a higher-level abstraction compared to the basic Producer and Consumer APIs, making it easier to build complex stream processing pipelines without the need for specialized processing frameworks. Key features of Kafka Streams include: Simple APIs for common stream processing operations including filtering, mapping, joining, aggregating, and windowing, Exactly-once processing semantics to ensure that each event is processed exactly one time, even in the case of failures or retries, Event-time processing with support for late-arriving events, Stateful processing with automatic state management and fault tolerance, Scalability through partitioning, where the library automatically parallelizes processing based on the partitions of the input topics, Fault tolerance through automatic recovery from failures, including the ability to resume processing from the correct offset, At-least-once delivery guarantees by default, with exactly-once delivery available when combined with idempotent producers and transactional writes, and Integration with Kafka's ecosystem, including Kafka Connect for data integration. Kafka Streams applications are lightweight and can be run in any environment that supports Java, including containers, virtual machines, or bare metal servers. Multiple instances of the same Kafka Streams application can run in parallel, automatically sharing the work based on partition assignment. The library provides DSL (Domain Specific Language) and Processor API for building applications. The DSL provides a declarative approach using high-level abstractions like KStream and KTable, while the Processor API provides a more low-level, imperative approach for building custom processing logic."
