    detailedAnswer: "A KTable in Kafka Streams is an abstraction that represents a changelog stream of updates for each key. It is one of the two primary abstractions in the Kafka Streams DSL, along with KStream. While a KStream represents a stream of events where each event is independent, a KTable represents a stream of updates where each update is a new value for a specific key. A KTable can be thought of as a combination of a table and a stream - it has the characteristics of a table (where each key has a current value) and a stream (where updates to that value arrive over time). KTables are created from Kafka topics that are configured for compaction (log.cleanup.policy=compact), where Kafka retains only the latest value for each key. When you create a KTable from a Kafka topic, Kafka Streams will read the topic and maintain an in-memory representation of the current value for each key. As new messages with the same key arrive, Kafka Streams will update the KTable with the new value. KTables support various operations including: filter (filter based on value), mapValues (transform values), join (join with other KTables based on keys), leftJoin (left join with other KTables), and aggregate (aggregate values). KTables can be created from Kafka topics using the builder.table() method, or they can be created from KStreams using various aggregation and join operations. When you write a KTable back to a Kafka topic, Kafka Streams will produce a changelog stream that contains only the updates, rather than the full state of the table. This makes KTables efficient for representing stateful data that changes over time."
