    detailedAnswer: "A KStream in Kafka Streams is an abstraction that represents an unbounded, append-only stream of events where each message is treated as an independent unit. It is one of the two primary abstractions in the Kafka Streams DSL, along with KTable. A KStream represents a stream of immutable events that are appended to one or more Kafka topics. Each event in a KStream has a key and a value, and the events are ordered within each partition of the underlying Kafka topics. KStreams are created from Kafka topics using the builder.stream() method, and they support various operations for processing the events, including: filter (filter based on key or value), map (transform keys and values), flatMap (transform to zero, one, or more events), mapValues (transform values only), filterNot (inverse filter), and branch (split into multiple KStreams based on predicates). KStreams can also be joined with other KStreams or KTables, aggregated, or written to other Kafka topics. KStreams are processed in a continuous, streaming fashion, with events being processed as they arrive. The processing can be stateless (where each event is processed independently) or stateful (where events are processed using state that is maintained and updated over time). KStreams can be used to implement various stream processing patterns, including filtering, transformation, enrichment, and routing. When you write a KStream to a Kafka topic, Kafka Streams will produce messages to the topic in the same format as the input, maintaining the key-value pairs and ordering within partitions."
